00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:05.370
So we live in an
increasingly complex world.

00:01:05.370 --> 00:01:07.390
In the context of
data analytics,

00:01:07.390 --> 00:01:12.900
this means your data probably
live in many different places.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:14.420
And every once in
a while, there'll

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:19.650
be a new data processing
framework that get announced.

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:22.830
And maybe once you're done
with your data processing,

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.960
there might be different places
you need to upload your data

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:26.800
processing results.

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:32.010
You eventually realize
that it's very unlikely

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:35.020
that you can finish all
your data processing needs

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:37.200
in a single system.

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:41.820
It's very common that you
have to stage data processing

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.700
jobs in different systems
together, and essentially put

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:47.790
them in a form of a workflow.

00:01:47.790 --> 00:01:51.810
And once you have a workflow,
then ultimately comes

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:55.920
the need of, you want to be
able to schedule that workflow.

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.140
You want to monitor
that workflow.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:03.460
Really, think about a way to
help you to reduce complexity,

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:05.100
it's through automation.

00:02:05.100 --> 00:02:07.710
And here, it's really
important that you

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:10.560
have a tool that could
offload and manage

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:13.713
all this complexity of
scheduling and monitoring

00:02:13.713 --> 00:02:14.755
workflows on your behalf.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:21.180
With that purpose
in mind, we designed

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.480
and developed Composer.

00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:27.960
It's built on top of the popular
open source Apache Airflow.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:31.680
And then helps you to
create workflows at ease,

00:02:31.680 --> 00:02:36.520
making it powerful for you
to manage your workflows.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:38.370
So exactly how
does Code Composer

00:02:38.370 --> 00:02:42.990
helps you to reduce
your workflow management

00:02:42.990 --> 00:02:45.990
complexities and help you
to improve your workflow

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:48.180
development?

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.480
We focus on a few areas.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.490
I mean, namely the first
one is really about,

00:02:53.490 --> 00:02:56.580
we want to make sure Composer
being in the orchestration

00:02:56.580 --> 00:03:00.157
choice when it comes to GCP.

00:03:00.157 --> 00:03:01.740
So what that means
is, we want to make

00:03:01.740 --> 00:03:04.200
sure that from
Cloud Composer, you

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:07.020
could orchestrate and
manage pretty much all

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.180
your GCP related workloads.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:14.430
[INAUDIBLE] jobs data
flow, data proc jobs.

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:15.977
Second thing, as I
mentioned earlier,

00:03:15.977 --> 00:03:17.310
we realized that it needed that.

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:19.650
You know, today
your data is likely

00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:24.450
going to live in different
places, on prem and on cloud.

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:29.790
So we designed Composer on
day one with that requirement

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:31.590
that whatever
orchestration solution

00:03:31.590 --> 00:03:33.680
we're going to introduce,
that needs to work well

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:35.910
in a hybrid cloud environment.

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:41.340
Also, be able to come up
with high abstractions,

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:45.458
and also make it really easy
for you to write your workflow.

00:03:45.458 --> 00:03:47.250
Especially now think
about if your workflow

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:51.990
has tens or hundreds
or thousands of tasks.

00:03:51.990 --> 00:03:54.173
Again, we want to make
it really easy for you

00:03:54.173 --> 00:03:55.340
to be able to act surprised.

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:59.310
Workflows consist of
that many of task.

00:03:59.310 --> 00:04:03.690
Finally, to increase
reusability.

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:06.472
Because you have done all
your development on prem,

00:04:06.472 --> 00:04:08.180
we don't want it to
be the case that when

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:11.340
you move to Google Cloud,
you realize, oh, now I need

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:14.290
to redevelop all my workflows.

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:18.570
So we really put open source
in the core of Cloud Composer.

00:04:18.570 --> 00:04:21.570
And we make sure that
whatever workflows you write

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:25.200
or you develop,
those can run in GCP.

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:29.610
And then likewise, when you move
certain workflows to on prem,

00:04:29.610 --> 00:04:31.680
you find out you don't
have to modify anything.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:35.490
Things should just
work out of the box.

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:37.890
So with those
requirements in mind,

00:04:37.890 --> 00:04:40.560
and then we added
a lot of more--

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:43.940
we started design and
development of Cloud Composer.

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:46.180
And then one of the
questions we often

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:50.370
got was, how does Cloud
Composer differentiate

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:52.200
with self-managed
Apache Airflow?

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.570
I mean, I understand
probably many of you

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:57.480
have on prem
Airflow deployments.

00:04:57.480 --> 00:05:02.070
And you're wondering, what are
some of the value added pieces

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:04.810
if I move to Cloud Composer?

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:06.150
There are quite some of those.

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:09.250
I'm going to probably
highlight a few important ones.

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:11.280
The first one is really
about-- a lot of those

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:15.270
are really sort of
center around simplicity,

00:05:15.270 --> 00:05:18.210
and also trying to
help you to minimize

00:05:18.210 --> 00:05:22.480
your operational complexities.

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:25.010
So we have really
convenient tooling.

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:29.130
Cloud Console, and
G Cloud for you

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:34.110
to manage your Cloud
Composer environment,

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:37.710
and make it easy for you
to upload your workflow

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:39.015
and inspect workflow status.

00:05:41.650 --> 00:05:45.400
Second thing, probably
70% to 80% of people

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:47.433
rely on the Airflow web UI.

00:05:47.433 --> 00:05:48.850
And also, frankly
speaking, that's

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:52.690
also one of the
distinct factors that

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:55.060
draw people to Apache Airflow.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:05:57.010
So we understand the need.

00:05:57.010 --> 00:06:00.660
That's why we put
a lot of thoughts

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:04.690
to make things easier for you
to host your Apache Airflow web

00:06:04.690 --> 00:06:07.510
server, and also make
it extremely secure

00:06:07.510 --> 00:06:12.080
through our cloud
Identity-Aware proxy.

00:06:12.080 --> 00:06:13.230
And then when it comes to--

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:15.460
Airflow has different versions.

00:06:15.460 --> 00:06:17.200
Some people choose
to-- there's always

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:20.850
different [INAUDIBLE]
versions, since Airflow

00:06:20.850 --> 00:06:22.780
is developing Python.

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:24.490
So again, in Cloud
Composer, we're

00:06:24.490 --> 00:06:26.970
really happy to
streamline and simplify

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:31.000
the management of Python
and Airflow versions.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:33.490
So that in Composer,
you can specify,

00:06:33.490 --> 00:06:35.140
and then which
Python and Airflow

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:37.870
version you want to use, and
then also open a path for you

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:41.500
to sort of upgrade
from one Airflow

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:43.480
to a different Airflow version.

00:06:43.480 --> 00:06:45.640
Given that it is often
the case that when

00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:47.670
you write your
workflow, you realize

00:06:47.670 --> 00:06:50.440
that you may have external
library dependencies,

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:52.900
you may have
customized operators.

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:55.270
So that's why we also
make it extremely simple

00:06:55.270 --> 00:07:01.180
for you to install and manage
additional PyPi dependencies.

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:04.290
Debugging and monitoring
are common needs these days.

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:05.890
[INAUDIBLE] workflow failures.

00:07:05.890 --> 00:07:09.600
So Composer comes out of
box with Stackdriver logging

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:11.230
and the monitoring integration.

00:07:11.230 --> 00:07:16.280
So whenever you have issues
regarding to a particular task,

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:20.770
you would be able to find
all the logs in Stackdriver.

00:07:20.770 --> 00:07:24.010
And finally, there
are IMs for you

00:07:24.010 --> 00:07:29.410
to sort of streamline
your composing environment

00:07:29.410 --> 00:07:31.030
management, and
manage permissions

00:07:31.030 --> 00:07:34.270
who has access to what.

00:07:34.270 --> 00:07:38.860
We launched Cloud Composer
last year during Google Next.

00:07:38.860 --> 00:07:42.680
It has probably been
about nine months.

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:47.270
And since then, we added
a lot more GCP operators.

00:07:47.270 --> 00:07:51.400
That's really why I
want to highlight here.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:54.400
As you can see from
the slide, pretty much

00:07:54.400 --> 00:07:59.300
we support all debug
of GCP operators.

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:03.790
You know, you have Machine Cloud
AI, Translate, Cloud Vision,

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:05.230
Kubernetes.

00:08:05.230 --> 00:08:09.760
On the data side, you have Cloud
SQL, BigTable, [INAUDIBLE],,

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:13.630
and Spanner.

00:08:13.630 --> 00:08:16.540
So those are all really
trying to make it easier

00:08:16.540 --> 00:08:18.383
for you to write workflows.

00:08:18.383 --> 00:08:20.050
So you don't really
have to worry about,

00:08:20.050 --> 00:08:24.260
how should I interact
with these new systems?

00:08:24.260 --> 00:08:26.860
I also want to, as I mention
that writing workflows are

00:08:26.860 --> 00:08:32.260
easy, and Airflow has a very
expressive mechanism for you

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:35.360
to express your workflow.

00:08:35.360 --> 00:08:37.179
So in the next couple
of slides, I just

00:08:37.179 --> 00:08:39.190
want to show you
a quick overview

00:08:39.190 --> 00:08:42.424
of how a DAG or a
workflow is developed

00:08:42.424 --> 00:08:44.230
in Composer or Airflow.

00:08:44.230 --> 00:08:47.400
So a little bit context, a
history of Apache Airflow.

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:51.860
It's a project that was
originally developed by Airbnb

00:08:51.860 --> 00:08:57.270
and subsequently got donated
to Apache Software Foundation.

00:08:57.270 --> 00:08:59.650
And since then, it has
gained a lot of popularity

00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:00.562
in the community.

00:09:00.562 --> 00:09:02.020
It's a very, very
active community.

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:06.820
It has a few hundred
contributors.

00:09:06.820 --> 00:09:11.200
Workflows are essentially
expressed as Directed Acyclic

00:09:11.200 --> 00:09:11.860
Graph.

00:09:11.860 --> 00:09:13.110
That's why you probably have--

00:09:13.110 --> 00:09:15.620
for people coming from
the Airflow community,

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:19.840
you probably have heard them
talking a lot about DAGs.

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:22.580
DAGs really essentially
[INAUDIBLE] workflows.

00:09:22.580 --> 00:09:28.630
This is an example DAG of how
the Hello world-ish workflow.

00:09:28.630 --> 00:09:32.170
It has about a three
task, and then you

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:33.790
run different commands.

00:09:33.790 --> 00:09:36.340
And then you have ways
to specify the dependency

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:39.670
among your task.

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:42.760
At the beginning, you
do your standard part,

00:09:42.760 --> 00:09:45.510
like standard Python imports.

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:49.720
Those really just
tell Airflow, hey, I

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:51.970
want to [INAUDIBLE]
the D-A-G and the DAG

00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:54.228
is the workflow, top
level workflow construct.

00:09:54.228 --> 00:09:56.770
And after that, it's just import
whatever operators you think

00:09:56.770 --> 00:09:57.760
are useful.

00:09:57.760 --> 00:09:59.920
In this case, we
use a bash operator,

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:02.620
but there are a Python
operator, and then

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:06.370
there's a lot of GCP
operators, such as say

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:08.350
run big query, query operator.

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:11.470
So many different
operators are there.

00:10:11.470 --> 00:10:13.030
Once you're done
with the import,

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:16.330
then you put all
your orchestration

00:10:16.330 --> 00:10:19.390
or scheduling arguments
there, like things

00:10:19.390 --> 00:10:22.720
like who are the owner
of this workflow,

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:24.310
how often do you
want to run, when

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:25.852
do you want to start
a workflow, when

00:10:25.852 --> 00:10:29.240
do you want to end the
workflow, so on and so forth.

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:32.560
Once you have the arguments set
up, then writing the workflow

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:35.440
itself is actually
really straightforward.

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:37.570
With the top line
DAG definition,

00:10:37.570 --> 00:10:42.160
then you can specify your
task, any task that you plan

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:44.450
to contain in that workflow.

00:10:44.450 --> 00:10:47.740
So as I mentioned in this
example, we use bash operator.

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:50.470
But you can, in fact,
use any operator.

00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:52.480
The nice thing
really about Airflow

00:10:52.480 --> 00:10:54.520
is, it has the
flexibility for you

00:10:54.520 --> 00:10:57.290
to introduce your own operator.

00:10:57.290 --> 00:11:03.040
This is really tying
back to my early point

00:11:03.040 --> 00:11:04.930
about it managing complexity.

00:11:04.930 --> 00:11:07.780
Be able to come out with
higher level abstractions,

00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:10.780
and be able to hide
that complexity

00:11:10.780 --> 00:11:14.500
from your internal users
is really one way for us

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:17.685
to navigate this complex
sort of workspace.

00:11:17.685 --> 00:11:19.060
Once you put
everything together,

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:23.110
you submit it to Cloud Composer.

00:11:23.110 --> 00:11:24.760
From that point
onwards, Composer

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:27.670
will take care of
scheduling, running,

00:11:27.670 --> 00:11:31.130
and monitoring your workflows.

00:11:31.130 --> 00:11:37.360
This is sort of like a
typical bash web UI for you

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:40.780
to access your workflow.

00:11:40.780 --> 00:11:45.060
From there, you would be able
to inspect the graphic structure

00:11:45.060 --> 00:11:47.080
of your workflow,
and also you would

00:11:47.080 --> 00:11:49.840
be able to inspect
different logs when

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:52.000
it comes to individual task.

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:54.470
They also have a rich
set of dashboarding

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:59.660
for you to inspect, if you
put all your task runs,

00:11:59.660 --> 00:12:04.180
workflow runs together, how does
the processing time fluctuates

00:12:04.180 --> 00:12:05.890
across different time runs?

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:09.160
Sometimes that helps
you to detect anomaly

00:12:09.160 --> 00:12:10.990
in your workflow.

00:12:10.990 --> 00:12:14.370
Behind the scene,
this is what happens.

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:18.920
Cloud Composer creates a
collection of GCP resources.

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:21.370
And then leveraging those
resources [INAUDIBLE]

00:12:21.370 --> 00:12:23.200
those resources are
collectively known

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:25.270
as cloud composing environment.

00:12:25.270 --> 00:12:28.000
And then within
that environment, we

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:31.540
host Airflow
scheduler worker web

00:12:31.540 --> 00:12:35.170
server the database, and
then the workflow storage.

00:12:35.170 --> 00:12:37.630
The same workflow storage
is also used for you

00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:40.000
to store workflow artifacts.

00:12:40.000 --> 00:12:42.250
And then once you have
your DAGs there, then

00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:44.410
you will be able to
orchestrate and connect

00:12:44.410 --> 00:12:47.140
to a host set of
external systems.

00:12:47.140 --> 00:12:49.160
Earlier I showed you
really a simple example,

00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:52.807
but then really that can
be as complicated as this.

00:12:52.807 --> 00:12:54.640
Although the graph
itself looks complicated,

00:12:54.640 --> 00:12:58.180
but actually the code behind
the graph isn't that hard.

00:12:58.180 --> 00:12:58.990
So this is--

00:12:58.990 --> 00:13:01.300
I just want to say that
this is a real DAG we

00:13:01.300 --> 00:13:05.710
use within the Cloud Composer
team to sort of dynamically

00:13:05.710 --> 00:13:08.500
generate Airflow docker images.

00:13:08.500 --> 00:13:11.800
That's so far about
a quick overview

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:14.030
of Composer and Airflow.

00:13:14.030 --> 00:13:18.700
Next I'll hand over to Daniel
to talk about Pandora's move

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:20.172
to Cloud Composer.

00:13:20.172 --> 00:13:21.380
DANIEL BARRETT: Good morning.

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:22.600
My name is Daniel Barrett.

00:13:22.600 --> 00:13:25.300
I'm a staff engineer at Pandora.

00:13:25.300 --> 00:13:29.290
I'm here to talk today about our
move to GCP with Cloud Composer

00:13:29.290 --> 00:13:32.470
from on prem Airflow.

00:13:32.470 --> 00:13:37.120
Just to give a little history
on the scheduling at Pandora.

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:39.240
Originally when I
started, we had a system

00:13:39.240 --> 00:13:41.120
of legacy Java schedulers.

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:42.940
And they were based in Java.

00:13:42.940 --> 00:13:47.530
They used the Quartz framework
as the actual scheduler.

00:13:47.530 --> 00:13:50.350
They were completely
developed in house.

00:13:50.350 --> 00:13:52.417
So because they were
developed in house,

00:13:52.417 --> 00:13:55.000
it was very hard for engineers,
analysts outside of analytics,

00:13:55.000 --> 00:13:57.580
to create jobs in the framework.

00:13:57.580 --> 00:14:00.820
There wasn't a whole lot
of online support either.

00:14:00.820 --> 00:14:04.540
It was all based off of whatever
we documented internally.

00:14:04.540 --> 00:14:06.795
It had a limited UI that
we developed ourselves.

00:14:06.795 --> 00:14:08.170
If we wanted to
add new features,

00:14:08.170 --> 00:14:10.003
we had to go out and
develop them ourselves.

00:14:10.003 --> 00:14:12.970
That also took place
when you're doing

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:14.360
other work and other tasks.

00:14:14.360 --> 00:14:19.150
So a lot of times it was very
slow to be developed in the UI.

00:14:19.150 --> 00:14:23.590
About two years ago, we started
our migration to Airflow.

00:14:23.590 --> 00:14:27.580
The Airflow UI is full of
features and incredibly easy

00:14:27.580 --> 00:14:29.400
to use.

00:14:29.400 --> 00:14:33.550
It has support, as Feng showed
you, for various technologies

00:14:33.550 --> 00:14:34.750
within Google Cloud.

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:37.600
And then also
regular technologies

00:14:37.600 --> 00:14:41.170
that allow you to integrate
with Hive and Hadoop and things

00:14:41.170 --> 00:14:44.560
like that we use to do
our analytics processing.

00:14:44.560 --> 00:14:47.830
It has a very large
online support community.

00:14:47.830 --> 00:14:49.780
If you run into
any problems, it's

00:14:49.780 --> 00:14:52.540
very easy to be able to
Google it and look it up,

00:14:52.540 --> 00:14:55.150
and somebody else has probably
run into that same problem,

00:14:55.150 --> 00:14:58.270
and will allow you to
be able to figure out

00:14:58.270 --> 00:15:01.150
a solution to your problem.

00:15:01.150 --> 00:15:03.970
It is all written in
Python and is incredibly

00:15:03.970 --> 00:15:06.310
easy to develop new
operators and sensors that

00:15:06.310 --> 00:15:08.980
will do custom tasks for you.

00:15:08.980 --> 00:15:11.170
Things that you do over and
over and over again, you

00:15:11.170 --> 00:15:13.840
can put those into operators and
allow other developers to use

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:15.550
them much more
simply and not have

00:15:15.550 --> 00:15:17.590
to reinvent the
wheel every time.

00:15:17.590 --> 00:15:19.810
Because it was open
source and there

00:15:19.810 --> 00:15:22.110
was a large community
behind it, it

00:15:22.110 --> 00:15:24.040
was very easy to onboard
new engineers that

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:26.410
had experience using Airflow.

00:15:26.410 --> 00:15:28.002
It was not a lot
of ramp up time.

00:15:28.002 --> 00:15:30.460
In our previous one, there was
quite a bit of ramp up time,

00:15:30.460 --> 00:15:33.910
and a period where people were
getting used to the legacy Java

00:15:33.910 --> 00:15:34.870
scheduler.

00:15:34.870 --> 00:15:38.107
That didn't have to
happen in Airflow.

00:15:38.107 --> 00:15:39.940
There was another issue
that we came across,

00:15:39.940 --> 00:15:43.120
and a lot of our
analysts at Pandora

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:46.660
would use Cron to be able
to schedule their Hive

00:15:46.660 --> 00:15:48.280
scripts in the past.

00:15:48.280 --> 00:15:52.000
This obviously is
not a great solution.

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:54.730
And it was very
easy for us to be

00:15:54.730 --> 00:15:58.570
able to integrate the analysts
to create Airflow tasks

00:15:58.570 --> 00:16:00.880
and jobs that could
depend on other jobs

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:02.860
and things of that nature.

00:16:02.860 --> 00:16:06.370
That made it much more
simpler and robust than just

00:16:06.370 --> 00:16:09.510
running a Cron
script using Hive.

00:16:09.510 --> 00:16:11.630
We were also able
to use a sensor

00:16:11.630 --> 00:16:13.310
to depend on other
analytics jobs

00:16:13.310 --> 00:16:15.680
completion outside of
your actual Airflow

00:16:15.680 --> 00:16:18.920
instance on prem.

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:20.390
After those two
years, now we have

00:16:20.390 --> 00:16:23.900
approximately about 90
instances between development

00:16:23.900 --> 00:16:26.280
and production in Pandora.

00:16:26.280 --> 00:16:31.260
Airflow has been adopted
very widely and very quickly.

00:16:31.260 --> 00:16:34.100
So there are five
problems that we looked at

00:16:34.100 --> 00:16:39.110
that we had to solve when we
moved from Airflow to Composer.

00:16:39.110 --> 00:16:41.377
One of them was
during the migration,

00:16:41.377 --> 00:16:42.960
Composer and Airflow
were both running

00:16:42.960 --> 00:16:45.560
in cloud and on prem, and the
issues between running them

00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:47.672
in two locations.

00:16:47.672 --> 00:16:49.130
There was also the
question of, how

00:16:49.130 --> 00:16:53.310
do we depend on a job
finishing in either location?

00:16:53.310 --> 00:16:57.560
And then Composer is a true
distributive framework.

00:16:57.560 --> 00:17:04.190
On prem, we made the decision to
deploy Composer to a single VM,

00:17:04.190 --> 00:17:07.490
so that it was a lot
simpler for the deployment,

00:17:07.490 --> 00:17:11.869
so that we did not have
to manage a distributed

00:17:11.869 --> 00:17:15.450
deployment of Airflow on prem.

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:17.640
And then how do we
support analysts

00:17:17.640 --> 00:17:20.520
in creating jobs
that run on dataproc?

00:17:20.520 --> 00:17:24.960
Back on prem, they were used
to having a Hadoop cluster that

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:27.620
was always on that they
could submit jobs to anytime.

00:17:27.620 --> 00:17:29.370
Now they had to figure
out how to actually

00:17:29.370 --> 00:17:31.900
run stuff on dataproc itself.

00:17:31.900 --> 00:17:33.450
And then finally,
how do we deploy

00:17:33.450 --> 00:17:38.940
code to Composer from an on prem
Bitbucket or a repository on

00:17:38.940 --> 00:17:40.230
prem?

00:17:40.230 --> 00:17:42.480
So the first one that we
solved was the job dependency

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:44.050
solution.

00:17:44.050 --> 00:17:47.340
So for that, we have a
Postgres database on prem.

00:17:47.340 --> 00:17:49.780
Eventually we'll move
that to the cloud.

00:17:49.780 --> 00:17:52.830
And there is a meta service
REST API container that

00:17:52.830 --> 00:17:54.690
sits in front of that
Postgres database

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:58.200
that handles requests for jobs
or tables that are finished

00:17:58.200 --> 00:18:03.510
or code that is asking, is
something finished for the day.

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:06.630
So that allows jobs or
tables to be set as complete

00:18:06.630 --> 00:18:10.050
for a specific day parameter.

00:18:10.050 --> 00:18:12.960
It would also allow you to get
the status of a job or table

00:18:12.960 --> 00:18:15.980
for a very specific day.

00:18:15.980 --> 00:18:18.860
And so around that, we
created an Airflow sensor

00:18:18.860 --> 00:18:21.800
that would ask the meta service
if a dependency is fulfilled.

00:18:21.800 --> 00:18:24.200
If you are depending
on job x, you

00:18:24.200 --> 00:18:26.480
would create that
sensor in your workflow.

00:18:26.480 --> 00:18:32.750
And it would say, is job x
done for April 1 of 2019.

00:18:32.750 --> 00:18:34.640
And then on the
flip side, we also

00:18:34.640 --> 00:18:38.600
created an operator that at the
end of the Airflow workflow,

00:18:38.600 --> 00:18:41.870
you could set your
dependency as complete--

00:18:41.870 --> 00:18:46.410
your table, your job, whatever
it is that you were running.

00:18:46.410 --> 00:18:50.070
So this is what that looks
like from the architecture.

00:18:50.070 --> 00:18:52.040
In here we have two
Composer instances

00:18:52.040 --> 00:18:57.050
that are running in GCP
and two Airflow on prem.

00:18:57.050 --> 00:19:00.000
There is the meta service
with the Postgres service,

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:02.580
Postgres database backing it.

00:19:02.580 --> 00:19:04.400
So for this instance,
we would have

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:07.760
a job populating a table running
on Composer instance number

00:19:07.760 --> 00:19:09.560
one.

00:19:09.560 --> 00:19:13.760
That job, the several jobs
that are waiting on that table

00:19:13.760 --> 00:19:17.270
to be done on Composer instance
number two and on prem three

00:19:17.270 --> 00:19:18.560
and four.

00:19:18.560 --> 00:19:21.680
After the table is
populated in Composer one,

00:19:21.680 --> 00:19:26.510
it would send a status
done to that meta service

00:19:26.510 --> 00:19:29.960
in that Postgres database.

00:19:29.960 --> 00:19:31.460
And then the meta
service would then

00:19:31.460 --> 00:19:34.580
tell two, three, and four
that that job is now complete.

00:19:34.580 --> 00:19:37.700
And then their workflows would
be able to move on from there.

00:19:37.700 --> 00:19:41.690
There are a couple of approach
drawbacks to this in GCP.

00:19:41.690 --> 00:19:45.230
One is that, because we are
Postgres instances on prem,

00:19:45.230 --> 00:19:48.140
and because we have
on prem instances,

00:19:48.140 --> 00:19:51.140
it is required that we need a
VPC network for our Composer

00:19:51.140 --> 00:19:53.240
instances in GCP.

00:19:53.240 --> 00:19:55.940
So currently, due to
our networking model

00:19:55.940 --> 00:20:01.940
that we decided in GCP,
we use shared VPCs.

00:20:01.940 --> 00:20:05.420
And currently you can
only have one shared VPC

00:20:05.420 --> 00:20:07.560
network per Composer instance.

00:20:07.560 --> 00:20:09.230
So in a specific
project, that means

00:20:09.230 --> 00:20:12.320
that we can only have one
Composer instance that uses

00:20:12.320 --> 00:20:15.440
that job dependency framework.

00:20:15.440 --> 00:20:19.130
And also, if you have a
table that needs historical,

00:20:19.130 --> 00:20:21.530
and for some reason it
happened before we set up

00:20:21.530 --> 00:20:24.680
this framework, and those
tables or those jobs

00:20:24.680 --> 00:20:26.900
are not done in that
framework, then we

00:20:26.900 --> 00:20:28.550
need some kind of
dependency override

00:20:28.550 --> 00:20:31.790
to be able to process history
where the dependencies are not

00:20:31.790 --> 00:20:34.070
set in the past.

00:20:34.070 --> 00:20:37.110
So the next problem we do
is dealing with Celery.

00:20:37.110 --> 00:20:40.500
So Celery is the asynchronous
task queue job scheduler used

00:20:40.500 --> 00:20:42.800
as the workers in Composer.

00:20:42.800 --> 00:20:46.290
And it has some drawbacks.

00:20:46.290 --> 00:20:48.890
So specifically,
I had a job that

00:20:48.890 --> 00:20:51.020
was a scientist job that
had three pipelines that

00:20:51.020 --> 00:20:52.562
were highly parallelized
that we were

00:20:52.562 --> 00:20:54.605
going to move to GCP last year.

00:20:54.605 --> 00:20:56.480
So I took these pipelines,
and I knew that it

00:20:56.480 --> 00:20:58.760
had three distinct parts.

00:20:58.760 --> 00:21:02.690
And I ran them manually
on specific machines

00:21:02.690 --> 00:21:04.490
to determine what
type of machine

00:21:04.490 --> 00:21:08.180
could handle each
individual task.

00:21:08.180 --> 00:21:10.580
From that, I determined
the actual machine time

00:21:10.580 --> 00:21:13.260
that I was going to use
to handle these pipelines.

00:21:13.260 --> 00:21:15.470
So originally what
I did is I set up

00:21:15.470 --> 00:21:19.160
Composer with three worker nodes
with the very specific machine

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:21.680
type that I knew I wanted to
be able to handle this task.

00:21:21.680 --> 00:21:25.340
It was an
n1-standard-16, 16 cores.

00:21:25.340 --> 00:21:28.930
When it came to actually
scheduling those tasks,

00:21:28.930 --> 00:21:31.910
the Celery scheduler calls
those nodes to be overwhelmed.

00:21:31.910 --> 00:21:34.400
Because what it would
do is it would actually

00:21:34.400 --> 00:21:37.880
schedule two tasks on
one node and one task

00:21:37.880 --> 00:21:40.380
on another node and then
nothing on the third node.

00:21:40.380 --> 00:21:44.370
It was not an even
scheduling of tasks itself.

00:21:44.370 --> 00:21:46.820
And because my node type
couldn't handle more than one

00:21:46.820 --> 00:21:52.660
of the pipelines, it
would cause it to freeze.

00:21:52.660 --> 00:21:55.600
So this is what it looked like
when it would actually execute.

00:21:55.600 --> 00:21:56.930
You would have the scheduler.

00:21:56.930 --> 00:22:00.250
It would execute one of them
on worker one, two of them

00:22:00.250 --> 00:22:03.250
on worker two, and nothing
running on worker three.

00:22:03.250 --> 00:22:05.890
So the solution that I'd
used to get around this

00:22:05.890 --> 00:22:09.460
is I created three VM instances
of the type of machine

00:22:09.460 --> 00:22:11.050
that I wanted to.

00:22:11.050 --> 00:22:13.540
And I named them processor
one, two, and three.

00:22:13.540 --> 00:22:17.210
And then I shut those
machine instances down.

00:22:17.210 --> 00:22:20.830
I then set up Composer
with three normal nodes,

00:22:20.830 --> 00:22:23.500
n1-standard-4.

00:22:23.500 --> 00:22:24.970
And then I set up
the Composer DAG

00:22:24.970 --> 00:22:28.750
to use those processor machines.

00:22:28.750 --> 00:22:31.660
And what I would do
is, my task would

00:22:31.660 --> 00:22:35.230
FCP fresh code from Composer
over to the processor

00:22:35.230 --> 00:22:36.860
machines themselves.

00:22:36.860 --> 00:22:40.030
And then it would use an SSH
command to run the pipeline

00:22:40.030 --> 00:22:42.890
on each individual instance.

00:22:42.890 --> 00:22:44.680
So this enabled the
actual processing

00:22:44.680 --> 00:22:47.410
to happen on the
processing machines

00:22:47.410 --> 00:22:50.420
and not on the
Composer nodes itself.

00:22:50.420 --> 00:22:52.930
So what would happen
there is my scheduler

00:22:52.930 --> 00:22:57.040
would schedule on worker
one and two on worker two,

00:22:57.040 --> 00:22:58.750
but then those
workers would actually

00:22:58.750 --> 00:23:02.450
execute the code on the actual
processor machines themselves.

00:23:02.450 --> 00:23:04.390
So I ended up with one
task running on one,

00:23:04.390 --> 00:23:07.210
one task running on two, and
one task running on three.

00:23:07.210 --> 00:23:10.700
This enabled the process to
happen the way that I actually

00:23:10.700 --> 00:23:12.280
wanted it to happen.

00:23:12.280 --> 00:23:14.980
At the end of the processing,
when everything was done,

00:23:14.980 --> 00:23:17.710
I would then shut down
processor one, two,

00:23:17.710 --> 00:23:19.480
and three so we were
no longer getting

00:23:19.480 --> 00:23:22.580
charged to use those nodes.

00:23:22.580 --> 00:23:24.963
This is what the
resulting DAG looked like.

00:23:24.963 --> 00:23:26.380
If you look at the
very beginning,

00:23:26.380 --> 00:23:28.780
you'll see a start
VM instances task.

00:23:28.780 --> 00:23:32.470
This is where I would start up
processor one, two, and three.

00:23:32.470 --> 00:23:36.640
And then I would wait for those
instances to fully start up.

00:23:36.640 --> 00:23:39.430
I then had tasks to
FCP the code over

00:23:39.430 --> 00:23:41.960
to each one of
those VM instances.

00:23:41.960 --> 00:23:43.690
And then I would
run the scripts.

00:23:43.690 --> 00:23:45.910
And then it would have a
couple of different tasks

00:23:45.910 --> 00:23:48.980
that would do some other
processing and some local data

00:23:48.980 --> 00:23:52.650
cleanup on each one
of those VM instances.

00:23:52.650 --> 00:23:56.020
And then at the very end,
you see at the very last one,

00:23:56.020 --> 00:23:58.870
I would stop all three
of those VM instances

00:23:58.870 --> 00:24:01.360
because we don't need
them running until they're

00:24:01.360 --> 00:24:03.380
ready to go the next day.

00:24:03.380 --> 00:24:05.560
So the next problem
we had to look at was,

00:24:05.560 --> 00:24:10.210
how do we make Dataproc
easier for analysts to use?

00:24:10.210 --> 00:24:13.240
Analysts, like I
said before, they're

00:24:13.240 --> 00:24:16.540
used to having a Hadoop
cluster that is always on

00:24:16.540 --> 00:24:18.370
and accepts their
jobs at any time.

00:24:18.370 --> 00:24:20.560
They may have to
wait for resources,

00:24:20.560 --> 00:24:22.060
but they could just
fire off queries

00:24:22.060 --> 00:24:25.840
and fire off tasks and send
them to this Hadoop cluster.

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:27.790
Dataproc is a little
bit different than that.

00:24:27.790 --> 00:24:29.373
Dataproc you have
to know a little bit

00:24:29.373 --> 00:24:31.130
to be able to create
a Dataproc instance.

00:24:31.130 --> 00:24:33.213
You have to understand it
a little bit more-- what

00:24:33.213 --> 00:24:36.040
are worker nodes,
some of the properties

00:24:36.040 --> 00:24:38.560
that are needed to start
up a Dataproc instance.

00:24:38.560 --> 00:24:40.120
So like I said, it
was a bar to entry

00:24:40.120 --> 00:24:44.180
for analysts to use the Dataproc
to run their schedule queries.

00:24:44.180 --> 00:24:48.880
So what we ended up developing
was a batch job framework.

00:24:48.880 --> 00:24:51.370
And this was developed with
a Google contractor called

00:24:51.370 --> 00:24:53.290
Neudesic.

00:24:53.290 --> 00:24:54.880
This was a batch
job framework that

00:24:54.880 --> 00:24:57.970
requires minimal coding
and really hardly

00:24:57.970 --> 00:25:00.460
any Dataproc
knowledge whatsoever.

00:25:00.460 --> 00:25:03.100
It would require them
to create config files

00:25:03.100 --> 00:25:06.315
that just defined their SQL,
the SQL tasks that they had,

00:25:06.315 --> 00:25:07.690
and the variables
that were going

00:25:07.690 --> 00:25:10.780
to be put into those SQL tasks.

00:25:10.780 --> 00:25:14.710
And then the DAG was dynamically
configured from those config

00:25:14.710 --> 00:25:17.520
files themselves.

00:25:17.520 --> 00:25:19.980
The config files would define
external and intertagged

00:25:19.980 --> 00:25:23.490
dependencies, sensors based
off of other jobs that

00:25:23.490 --> 00:25:27.000
were running on other
Airflow instances on prem,

00:25:27.000 --> 00:25:29.530
the piece that I
showed you earlier.

00:25:29.530 --> 00:25:32.670
You would define those in the
config files for your SQL.

00:25:32.670 --> 00:25:34.950
And you would also
define inside of the DAG

00:25:34.950 --> 00:25:38.190
what SQL files
depended on other ones

00:25:38.190 --> 00:25:41.280
so that they could then be
set up correctly in a DAG.

00:25:41.280 --> 00:25:44.460
It would also provide a way to
instantiate a Dataproc cluster

00:25:44.460 --> 00:25:47.610
for each SQL statement run
using only the number of workers

00:25:47.610 --> 00:25:49.350
needed.

00:25:49.350 --> 00:25:51.915
And to do this,
there was a heavy use

00:25:51.915 --> 00:25:55.940
of Dataproc workflow templates.

00:25:55.940 --> 00:25:58.260
The Dataproc workflow
templates would start up

00:25:58.260 --> 00:26:00.780
a cluster for the batch job.

00:26:00.780 --> 00:26:03.540
They would run the SQL
file on that cluster.

00:26:03.540 --> 00:26:06.310
And then they would delete
that cluster afterwards.

00:26:06.310 --> 00:26:09.420
And we found that this was
the best way to use Dataproc.

00:26:09.420 --> 00:26:13.020
It was repeatable and
was less prone to error

00:26:13.020 --> 00:26:14.610
than some of the
other ways that we

00:26:14.610 --> 00:26:18.340
looked at running Dataproc and
running SQL files on Dataproc.

00:26:18.340 --> 00:26:21.990
This was all the code
that was required

00:26:21.990 --> 00:26:28.440
to be able to create
this dynamic DAG.

00:26:28.440 --> 00:26:32.640
So this create
batch DAG operator

00:26:32.640 --> 00:26:35.370
was what actually went through
all of the config files

00:26:35.370 --> 00:26:37.830
and constructed the DAG itself.

00:26:37.830 --> 00:26:41.890
This code can be copied from
one project to the next one.

00:26:41.890 --> 00:26:43.440
So the analysts
would not really need

00:26:43.440 --> 00:26:47.880
to know any Python whatsoever in
order to run their batch jobs.

00:26:47.880 --> 00:26:48.820
This could be high.

00:26:48.820 --> 00:26:49.740
This could be Spark.

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:53.120
And several of the different
ways of running stuff.

00:26:53.120 --> 00:26:57.680
From that code came
this DAG right here.

00:26:57.680 --> 00:27:00.050
So this DAG was created
using that code,

00:27:00.050 --> 00:27:03.887
using config files that
were set up for it.

00:27:03.887 --> 00:27:05.720
Like I said, it was a
minimal amount of code

00:27:05.720 --> 00:27:08.520
to be able to create
something as complex as this.

00:27:08.520 --> 00:27:12.590
This is one of our largest
jobs, called our nightly job.

00:27:12.590 --> 00:27:15.110
And it's a great name.

00:27:15.110 --> 00:27:17.365
But there is a lot of--

00:27:17.365 --> 00:27:18.740
you'll see on the
left, there are

00:27:18.740 --> 00:27:21.110
all of our external
sensors, that

00:27:21.110 --> 00:27:24.440
were other jobs on other Airflow
or imports or things like that,

00:27:24.440 --> 00:27:26.540
that we were waiting on
to be complete in order

00:27:26.540 --> 00:27:29.840
for our process to start.

00:27:29.840 --> 00:27:31.880
Each one of those
pieces would have--

00:27:31.880 --> 00:27:34.760
there would be data
quality checks involved

00:27:34.760 --> 00:27:37.370
in that, where after the
table was completely loaded,

00:27:37.370 --> 00:27:40.370
it would do duplicate checks
or threshold checks or things

00:27:40.370 --> 00:27:41.450
of that nature.

00:27:41.450 --> 00:27:44.240
So that you would ensure
that your data was

00:27:44.240 --> 00:27:46.590
up to your standards at
the end of the process.

00:27:46.590 --> 00:27:48.007
And then at the
very end, it would

00:27:48.007 --> 00:27:52.010
set its status as complete
for each individual table

00:27:52.010 --> 00:27:54.680
back in our analytics
Postgres database

00:27:54.680 --> 00:27:56.815
and allow other jobs to
be kicked off from that.

00:27:56.815 --> 00:27:58.190
So one of the
other ones was, how

00:27:58.190 --> 00:28:02.540
do we deploy code from on
prem Pandora to Composer

00:28:02.540 --> 00:28:04.280
in the cloud?

00:28:04.280 --> 00:28:06.830
So the ways that we came up
with to deploy, one of them

00:28:06.830 --> 00:28:08.810
would be an on
prem Jenkins build

00:28:08.810 --> 00:28:10.790
that uses a plug-in
that Google has

00:28:10.790 --> 00:28:14.240
for Jenkins to be able to push
code to a Composer DAG bucket.

00:28:14.240 --> 00:28:18.320
All of your code in Composer
is synced to a bucket.

00:28:18.320 --> 00:28:21.680
So whenever you create
a Composer instance,

00:28:21.680 --> 00:28:25.620
there is a Composer bucket that
is assigned to that instance.

00:28:25.620 --> 00:28:28.850
Inside of there, there is
a DAGs folder, and also

00:28:28.850 --> 00:28:29.935
a plugins folder.

00:28:29.935 --> 00:28:31.310
These are the two
places that you

00:28:31.310 --> 00:28:34.820
need to deploy your code in
order to run them in Composer.

00:28:34.820 --> 00:28:36.830
You can also just
drag and drop manually

00:28:36.830 --> 00:28:39.620
into the Composer bucket
using Google's console UI.

00:28:39.620 --> 00:28:42.650
That is a very simple way,
but it's also a manual way.

00:28:42.650 --> 00:28:45.080
So the other one would be
more of an automated build.

00:28:45.080 --> 00:28:49.220
The second one would be
more of a manual process.

00:28:49.220 --> 00:28:50.990
But something we came
up with at Pandora,

00:28:50.990 --> 00:28:54.290
because a lot of our
analysts are not really

00:28:54.290 --> 00:28:55.950
used to command
line performance,

00:28:55.950 --> 00:28:58.340
and so we came up
with a view that

00:28:58.340 --> 00:29:02.360
would allow them to
use a web page to sync

00:29:02.360 --> 00:29:05.330
a project from Bitbucket.

00:29:05.330 --> 00:29:07.440
This view looks like this.

00:29:07.440 --> 00:29:09.770
And this view when
you pulled it up

00:29:09.770 --> 00:29:13.130
would go out to our
internal Bitbucket repo.

00:29:13.130 --> 00:29:16.640
And will pull all of
the internal Pandora

00:29:16.640 --> 00:29:19.610
publicly available
Bitbucket repositories.

00:29:19.610 --> 00:29:21.435
You can select which
one you wanted.

00:29:21.435 --> 00:29:23.060
You could select the
branch you wanted,

00:29:23.060 --> 00:29:25.102
and then individually you
could select the commit

00:29:25.102 --> 00:29:26.180
that you wanted.

00:29:26.180 --> 00:29:27.620
And then when you
push Submit, it

00:29:27.620 --> 00:29:31.610
would sync that code to the
DAGs folder on the server

00:29:31.610 --> 00:29:33.590
that it was running
on on Pandora.

00:29:33.590 --> 00:29:35.900
So we had to take this
and move it to Composer

00:29:35.900 --> 00:29:39.060
to be able to do the
same kind of thing.

00:29:39.060 --> 00:29:41.332
So on prem, the view would
build the git command

00:29:41.332 --> 00:29:43.040
and then sync directly
to the file system

00:29:43.040 --> 00:29:46.070
because it would run the git
command as an external process

00:29:46.070 --> 00:29:47.190
in Python.

00:29:47.190 --> 00:29:50.240
On Composer, the
web server is not

00:29:50.240 --> 00:29:52.890
running on the Kubernetes
Composer cluster.

00:29:52.890 --> 00:29:55.430
So the web server doesn't
have access to the file system

00:29:55.430 --> 00:29:57.980
where your DAGs are
located or the data

00:29:57.980 --> 00:30:00.380
directory that you're allowed
to do work in on Composer

00:30:00.380 --> 00:30:01.760
is located.

00:30:01.760 --> 00:30:04.010
So we had to think of it a
little bit differently way.

00:30:04.010 --> 00:30:07.370
And we had to create a DAG to
take care of any web server

00:30:07.370 --> 00:30:10.880
action, and then send the DAG
arguments from the web server

00:30:10.880 --> 00:30:12.240
itself.

00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:15.400
That is because of this Composer
architecture right here.

00:30:15.400 --> 00:30:18.090
You'll see that the workers, the
Redis, the Airflow scheduler,

00:30:18.090 --> 00:30:20.490
and the Cloud SQL proxy are
all running on the Kubernetes

00:30:20.490 --> 00:30:21.390
Engine.

00:30:21.390 --> 00:30:26.160
But the Airflow web server is
running on the tenant project.

00:30:26.160 --> 00:30:30.000
So it did not have access to the
file system on the Kubernetes

00:30:30.000 --> 00:30:32.330
cluster itself.

00:30:32.330 --> 00:30:35.150
The downsides to a DAG sync
are the Composer bucket

00:30:35.150 --> 00:30:38.970
is syncing down to the container
file system, but not back up.

00:30:38.970 --> 00:30:42.470
So you could not just sync the
code to the actual local file

00:30:42.470 --> 00:30:45.200
system where the DAGs are
located because it would

00:30:45.200 --> 00:30:47.150
be overwritten the
next time that Google

00:30:47.150 --> 00:30:52.197
synced your code from the bucket
down to the file system itself.

00:30:52.197 --> 00:30:53.780
Another one is that
Bitbucket projects

00:30:53.780 --> 00:30:55.670
must be made public
within the organization

00:30:55.670 --> 00:30:58.740
in order to view them
to pull a list of them.

00:30:58.740 --> 00:31:01.610
Pulls, because of the network
from on prem to bucket,

00:31:01.610 --> 00:31:02.737
are a little slow.

00:31:02.737 --> 00:31:04.820
This is to be expected
because you're pulling them

00:31:04.820 --> 00:31:05.737
up to Google Composer.

00:31:08.480 --> 00:31:12.770
You have to sync the project
code to home Airflow GCS data

00:31:12.770 --> 00:31:16.880
before doing an rsync up
to the Composer bucket.

00:31:16.880 --> 00:31:20.240
The home Airflow GCS data
was just a temporary location

00:31:20.240 --> 00:31:22.350
in order to sync the code there.

00:31:22.350 --> 00:31:25.010
So we did not have to overwrite
everything in the bucket

00:31:25.010 --> 00:31:28.780
every time that we wanted to
do a pull from the bucket.

00:31:31.760 --> 00:31:33.870
And that is it for me.

00:31:33.870 --> 00:31:37.140
And I will hand you
over right now, sir.

00:31:37.140 --> 00:31:38.510
WILSON LIAN: Hi, everybody.

00:31:38.510 --> 00:31:39.240
I'm Wilson Lian.

00:31:39.240 --> 00:31:41.910
I'm a software engineer on
the Cloud Composer team.

00:31:41.910 --> 00:31:44.180
And today I have the
privilege of telling you

00:31:44.180 --> 00:31:47.270
all about some new features
that we've rolled out recently

00:31:47.270 --> 00:31:48.680
in Cloud Composer.

00:31:48.680 --> 00:31:51.710
So something that we've heard
a lot from our customers is,

00:31:51.710 --> 00:31:53.630
how can I monitor
my environments

00:31:53.630 --> 00:31:56.870
to make sure that they're
up and running and healthy

00:31:56.870 --> 00:32:00.260
and making progress on
DAGs, as well as determine

00:32:00.260 --> 00:32:02.720
whether or not I've
provisioned enough resources

00:32:02.720 --> 00:32:06.500
for my environment to
make progress-- or sorry,

00:32:06.500 --> 00:32:09.350
to handle the workload
that I'm throwing at it?

00:32:09.350 --> 00:32:12.950
And so to answer that question,
our Composer data plane

00:32:12.950 --> 00:32:16.160
is now exporting metrics
into Stackdriver.

00:32:16.160 --> 00:32:18.350
As you see here, we're
exporting information

00:32:18.350 --> 00:32:22.910
about the quantity and latency
of both DAG runs and task

00:32:22.910 --> 00:32:24.000
instances.

00:32:24.000 --> 00:32:26.270
So you can see if
anything has gotten stuck.

00:32:26.270 --> 00:32:29.680
We're also exporting information
about the health of the Celery

00:32:29.680 --> 00:32:32.390
queue, including the number
of Celery workers that are up

00:32:32.390 --> 00:32:34.380
and the length of the queue.

00:32:34.380 --> 00:32:36.770
Now let's flip over
to the demo screen,

00:32:36.770 --> 00:32:39.200
and I'll show you how you
can use these metrics.

00:32:39.200 --> 00:32:42.740
OK, so you can see here, I
built this dashboard earlier.

00:32:42.740 --> 00:32:46.220
Basically we're showing
on the left column

00:32:46.220 --> 00:32:51.260
the number of DAG runs
and task runs over time.

00:32:51.260 --> 00:32:53.960
And on the right column, we
have the latency of DAG runs

00:32:53.960 --> 00:32:56.400
and task runs over time.

00:32:56.400 --> 00:32:58.340
So if we zoom in
on the one hour,

00:32:58.340 --> 00:33:00.500
we can see that we have
four DAG runs per hour

00:33:00.500 --> 00:33:03.440
for a single particular DAG.

00:33:03.440 --> 00:33:05.000
Called sometimes slow.

00:33:05.000 --> 00:33:07.340
And we can see on
the right hand side,

00:33:07.340 --> 00:33:10.055
that the latency of
that DAG run varies

00:33:10.055 --> 00:33:11.180
over the course of an hour.

00:33:11.180 --> 00:33:13.820
In fact, once
every hour it takes

00:33:13.820 --> 00:33:18.030
much longer than the other
DAG runs in that hour.

00:33:18.030 --> 00:33:20.360
And if we look
down at the task, I

00:33:20.360 --> 00:33:23.210
happen to know that
this DAG has two tasks.

00:33:23.210 --> 00:33:24.920
One of them is
called consistent1.

00:33:24.920 --> 00:33:26.030
It's a bash operator.

00:33:26.030 --> 00:33:28.540
And it runs fairly consistently.

00:33:28.540 --> 00:33:30.290
And the other one is
called sometimesslow,

00:33:30.290 --> 00:33:31.670
and it's a Python operator.

00:33:31.670 --> 00:33:34.010
And I cheated for this
demo, and I made it

00:33:34.010 --> 00:33:36.950
so that sometimesslow
checks the time,

00:33:36.950 --> 00:33:39.380
and once an hour makes
itself one minute slower

00:33:39.380 --> 00:33:42.263
than every other
time that it runs.

00:33:42.263 --> 00:33:43.930
So this is a bit of
a contrived example,

00:33:43.930 --> 00:33:46.400
but you could see how, if you
had some external dependency

00:33:46.400 --> 00:33:48.735
that was introducing
latency into your DAG runs,

00:33:48.735 --> 00:33:51.110
you would be able to see this
in a Stackdriver monitoring

00:33:51.110 --> 00:33:52.310
console.

00:33:52.310 --> 00:33:53.840
And additionally,
you could go in

00:33:53.840 --> 00:33:57.383
and create an alerting policy.

00:33:57.383 --> 00:33:58.550
And let's do that right now.

00:33:58.550 --> 00:34:09.719
We'll create an alert so that
whenever that task is slow,

00:34:09.719 --> 00:34:11.040
we get an email.

00:34:11.040 --> 00:34:15.920
So we'll filter that
by the task name

00:34:15.920 --> 00:34:24.949
and say whenever sometimesslow
takes longer than 60 seconds,

00:34:24.949 --> 00:34:28.639
for one minute we
will get an email

00:34:28.639 --> 00:34:30.860
or some other notification.

00:34:30.860 --> 00:34:34.219
This is just standard
Stackdriver monitoring stuff.

00:34:34.219 --> 00:34:36.412
And then we can save that.

00:34:36.412 --> 00:34:38.120
And likewise, we could
do a similar thing

00:34:38.120 --> 00:34:41.150
for the Celery queue.

00:34:41.150 --> 00:34:44.730
Normally a Celery queue
length of zero is a good sign.

00:34:44.730 --> 00:34:46.730
That means that your
workers are able to keep up

00:34:46.730 --> 00:34:48.150
with the offered load.

00:34:48.150 --> 00:34:50.360
So we could set up an
alert on the Celery queue.

00:34:50.360 --> 00:34:53.150
And whenever it exceeds some
threshold, we can get an alert

00:34:53.150 --> 00:34:57.720
and realize that we need
to do some reprovisioning.

00:34:57.720 --> 00:35:02.010
So now let's swap back
to the slides, please.

00:35:02.010 --> 00:35:04.040
So the next feature that
I'm going to talk about

00:35:04.040 --> 00:35:05.990
is one that you can
use to help improve

00:35:05.990 --> 00:35:08.870
the security of your Cloud
Composer environments.

00:35:08.870 --> 00:35:11.870
So whenever you create a
Cloud Composer environment,

00:35:11.870 --> 00:35:15.350
as Dan pointed out, the
Cloud Composer control plane

00:35:15.350 --> 00:35:20.180
is going to create a GKE cluster
and Cloud SQL instance for you.

00:35:20.180 --> 00:35:25.400
And by default, the VMs for
the GKE cluster and Cloud SQL

00:35:25.400 --> 00:35:28.460
instance will have public
IPs, meaning that they

00:35:28.460 --> 00:35:30.380
are externally routable.

00:35:30.380 --> 00:35:33.440
And you can be reached from
anyone on the internet.

00:35:33.440 --> 00:35:36.680
Moreover, the communication
to your Airflow database

00:35:36.680 --> 00:35:39.930
in the Cloud SQL instance will
traverse the public internet.

00:35:39.930 --> 00:35:43.760
So what this means is,
your traffic potentially

00:35:43.760 --> 00:35:45.270
traverses the public internet.

00:35:45.270 --> 00:35:47.630
And if you have a
zero day vulnerability

00:35:47.630 --> 00:35:50.420
in some daemon running
on your system,

00:35:50.420 --> 00:35:53.400
it could be exploited by
someone on the internet.

00:35:53.400 --> 00:35:55.250
However, with
private IP clusters,

00:35:55.250 --> 00:35:58.970
we only assign internal
non-publicly routable IP

00:35:58.970 --> 00:36:03.800
addresses to all of your GKE VMs
and the Cloud SQL instance VM.

00:36:03.800 --> 00:36:06.510
Optionally, you can
enable a feature

00:36:06.510 --> 00:36:08.900
which will put the Kubernetes
master for your GKE

00:36:08.900 --> 00:36:12.087
cluster behind a
private only IP as well.

00:36:12.087 --> 00:36:13.670
So let's swap back
to the demo screen,

00:36:13.670 --> 00:36:16.370
and I'll show you how
you can create this.

00:36:16.370 --> 00:36:20.060
You can do this using the beta
channel of the G Cloud tool.

00:36:20.060 --> 00:36:22.910
But you can also do
this in the GCP console

00:36:22.910 --> 00:36:26.300
by enabling beta features
up in the top right corner.

00:36:26.300 --> 00:36:28.820
Let's just look at what a
private IP cluster looks like.

00:36:28.820 --> 00:36:33.390
OK, so I precreated this
private IP cluster earlier.

00:36:33.390 --> 00:36:37.130
You can see that a private
environment is set to true,

00:36:37.130 --> 00:36:39.860
and I have master external
access set to false.

00:36:39.860 --> 00:36:44.250
So the Kubernetes master should
only have an internal IP.

00:36:44.250 --> 00:36:46.790
So if we drill down
into the GKE cluster

00:36:46.790 --> 00:36:51.350
that's associated
with this, we can

00:36:51.350 --> 00:37:00.640
see that the master endpoint
has an IP address, 172.16.0.2,

00:37:00.640 --> 00:37:03.110
which is an internal IP address.

00:37:03.110 --> 00:37:05.320
And if we look at the
node pool to check out

00:37:05.320 --> 00:37:09.030
the GCE VMs that were
allocated for this,

00:37:09.030 --> 00:37:11.550
we see that they
have no external IPs.

00:37:11.550 --> 00:37:14.830
The last feature that
I like to touch on

00:37:14.830 --> 00:37:19.952
is one that we've been
working on for a long time.

00:37:19.952 --> 00:37:21.910
And basically, let's say
you're really excited,

00:37:21.910 --> 00:37:24.180
when you get out of here, to
go and try out the Stackdriver

00:37:24.180 --> 00:37:26.097
monitoring feature or
some of the new features

00:37:26.097 --> 00:37:29.350
that you've seen us
announce in recent releases.

00:37:29.350 --> 00:37:33.480
In the past, we would have asked
you to create a new environment

00:37:33.480 --> 00:37:35.230
and then move all your
DAGs over, and then

00:37:35.230 --> 00:37:39.430
move all your Airflow metadata
over to the new environment,

00:37:39.430 --> 00:37:41.590
and then take down
the old environment.

00:37:41.590 --> 00:37:43.940
Now, with just the
push of a button,

00:37:43.940 --> 00:37:47.740
you can upgrade your
environments in place

00:37:47.740 --> 00:37:50.380
and preserve the VMs, the
Cloud SQL instance, and all

00:37:50.380 --> 00:37:53.260
your Airflow metadata
on the same environment,

00:37:53.260 --> 00:37:56.200
while still running the
latest version of Composer

00:37:56.200 --> 00:37:59.860
or even an upgraded
version of Airflow.

00:37:59.860 --> 00:38:01.810
I can try to demo
this, but we'll

00:38:01.810 --> 00:38:04.575
see if we're able to try that.

00:38:04.575 --> 00:38:05.950
Let's go back to
the demo screen.

00:38:05.950 --> 00:38:08.920
So I have this environment here
that's ready to be upgraded.

00:38:08.920 --> 00:38:10.610
I've enabled beta features.

00:38:10.610 --> 00:38:15.580
And we can see that it's running
Composer 1.3.0 and Airflow

00:38:15.580 --> 00:38:17.090
1.9.0.

00:38:17.090 --> 00:38:20.230
So if we just hit the
Upgrade Image Version button,

00:38:20.230 --> 00:38:21.760
we can select a
new image version.

00:38:21.760 --> 00:38:24.710
1.6.1 is our most
recent version.

00:38:24.710 --> 00:38:28.160
And let's upgrade to the
latest version of Airflow.

00:38:28.160 --> 00:38:29.210
Submit.

00:38:29.210 --> 00:38:32.340
And then the upgrades
is in-flight.

00:38:32.340 --> 00:38:33.710
It'll complete in a few minutes.

00:38:33.710 --> 00:38:37.500
And then you'll be at
the latest version.

00:38:37.500 --> 00:38:39.810
That's all I have.

00:38:39.810 --> 00:38:42.400
For more information on
Cloud Composer or Airflow,

00:38:42.400 --> 00:38:43.650
you can check out these links.

00:38:44.550 --> 00:38:47.900
[MUSIC PLAYING]

